.Dd July 10, 2026
.Dt XTC_TAIL 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_tail_enable ,
.Nm xtc_tail_disable ,
.Nm xtc_tail_reset ,
.Nm xtc_tail_read ,
.Nm xtc_tail_dump ,
.Nm xtc_tail_count
.Nd runtime microscope (individual scheduler-event recording)
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libxtc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc_tail.h
.Ft unsigned
.Fn xtc_tail_enable "unsigned source_mask"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_tail_disable "void"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_tail_reset "void"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_tail_read "xtc_tail_fn cb" "void *user"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_tail_dump "int fd"
.Ft size_t
.Fn xtc_tail_count "void"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm xtc_tail
is a runtime microscope: it records every INDIVIDUAL runtime event,
tied to a precise instant, into a bounded ring -- so a degraded window
can be diffed against a normal one after the fact.  It is the
event-level complement to
.Xr xtc_stats 3
(aggregate counters) and
.Xr xtc_trace 3
(the causal message trace).  Inspired by dial9, "a microscope for
Tokio".
.Pp
Recording is OFF by default and costs a single predictable branch when
disabled, so it is safe to compile in for production and turn on only
while diagnosing.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_tail_enable
turns on the named event sources -- a bitwise OR of
.Dv XTC_TAIL_SCHED
(proc spawn / exit / wake / run),
.Dv XTC_TAIL_MSG ,
.Dv XTC_TAIL_IO ,
and
.Dv XTC_TAIL_OS
(the latter three reserved for the staged build-out).  The mask REPLACES
the current one and the previous mask is returned.
.Fn xtc_tail_disable
turns off all recording.
.Fn xtc_tail_reset
drops all buffered records.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_tail_read
visits every buffered record, oldest first, via
.Fa cb
(return nonzero to stop early); the snapshot is stable.
.Fn xtc_tail_count
returns how many records are buffered.
.Fn xtc_tail_dump
writes the buffer to
.Fa fd
as a versioned, compact, PORTABLE binary trace: an
.Vt xtc_tail_hdr_t
header
.Pq magic, version, endianness flag, count, base timestamp
written little-endian, followed by one varint/delta-encoded frame per
event (kind, source, LEB128 timestamp delta, pid, and detail).  The
delta-encoded timestamps and varint fields keep it to a handful of
bytes per event -- and being explicit little-endian with no struct
padding, a trace dumps and decodes identically across architectures --
for a separate offline tool to
render.
.Pp
Each
.Vt xtc_tail_rec_t
carries a monotonic timestamp, the source and kind, the proc pid, and a
kind-specific detail (an EXIT reason, or the wake-to-run latency in
nanoseconds for a RUN event -- the signal that exposes lost or late
wakeups).
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_tail_enable
returns the previously enabled source mask.
.Fn xtc_tail_reset ,
.Fn xtc_tail_read ,
and
.Fn xtc_tail_dump
return
.Dv XTC_OK
or a negative
.Dv XTC_E_*
code.
.Fn xtc_tail_count
returns the buffered record count.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_tail_disable
returns no value.
.Sh NOTES
Phase 1 implements the SCHED source (proc spawn and exit), the
in-process read, and the binary dump.  The MSG / IO / OS sources, an
on-disk spill with rotation, and the offline viewer are staged
follow-ons; the record layout and the source mask are stable so they
extend the format without a break.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xtc_tail_enable(XTC_TAIL_SCHED);
/* run the workload */
int fd = open("trace.xtcl", O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC, 0644);
xtc_tail_dump(fd);          /* versioned binary, for the offline viewer */
close(fd);
xtc_tail_disable();
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_stats 3 ,
.Xr xtc_trace 3 ,
.Xr xtc_inspect 3 ,
.Xr xtc 7
